    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-2-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Balanced, clean data yields the best classification results</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>true</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Clean, balanced data gives better results. If most of the data is one class, a model predicts that class more often.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>false</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. You clean and balance data to get better results. If most of the data is one class, a model predicts that class more often.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-2-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>How do you choose the right classifier?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Understand which classifiers work best for which scenarios</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A cheat sheet of which classifier fits which problem helps. But trying several and comparing results works too.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Educated guess and check</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Trying several classifiers is one way to test. A cheat sheet that matches classifiers to problems also helps.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Both of the above</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. You can try several classifiers and compare results, or use a cheat sheet that matches classifiers to problems.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-2-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Classification is a type of</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>NLP</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Scikit-learn groups classification under supervised learning, not NLP.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Supervised Learning</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Scikit-learn groups classification under supervised learning.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Programming language</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Classification is a kind of machine learning task, grouped under supervised learning.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-2-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What is a 'solver'?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the person who double-checks your work</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A solver is not a person. It is a setting that names the algorithm to use.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the algorithm to use in the optimization problem</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. The solver is defined as the algorithm to use in the optimization problem.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a machine learning technique</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A solver is a parameter you pass to logistic regression. Its value says which algorithm to use.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-2-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Which classifier did we use in this lesson?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Logistic Regression</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. This lesson trained a logistic regression model to predict a cuisine.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Decision Trees</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A decision tree might work, but this lesson used logistic regression.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>One-vs-All Multiclass</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. This lesson trained a logistic regression model. Its one-vs-rest ('ovr') option was only a setting, not the classifier.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-2-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>How do you know if the classification algorithm is working as expected?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>By checking the accuracy of its predictions</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. You score the model's predictions on test data and check the accuracy, which was over 80% here.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>By checking it against other algorithms</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Trying several classifiers helps you choose one. To know if yours works, check how accurate its predictions are.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>By looking at historical data for how good this algorithm is at solving similar problems</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. You check your own model on test data you held back, and look at its accuracy.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
